welcome back okay so we've been talking  about the dynamic mode decomposition  which is a powerful data-driven  technique to identify spatial-temporal  coherent structures and a linear model  for how they evolve in time this works  for very high dimensional complex data  and now I just want to walk you through  some of the key extensions and  applications and give you a quick  example of this okay so one of the  really nice things about DMD this is a  figure I made a while back so dynamic  mode decomposition first of all has a  very strong connection to Koopman  analysis so even though we're getting  these linear dynamical systems models  there's some connection to strongly  nonlinear dynamics through the Koopman  operator so the best fit linear DMD  model and the eigenvalues and  eigenvectors are related to I gain  values and eigen vectors of the Koopman  operator and because we formulated DMD  in a linear regression framework because  this is literally just a best fit linear  regression there are tons of extensions  that exist for DMD so it's highly  extensible you can add in your favorite  kind of technique into this DMD Koopman  framework pretty easily just because of  linear regression so this is an old  figure I'm just going to tell you some  of them I put a W here because these are  a lot of the ones that we're working on  there's extensions to DMD for systems  with incomplete measurements so if you  have spatially under resolved  measurements you can use compressed  sensing to reconstruct what the flow  fields are you can also do compressed  sensing in time if you don't have time  resolved snapshots Nathan Cutts  developed a multi resolution version of  DMD so instead of doing one big linear  regression on a long time scale you  essentially can do this Multi resolution  in time DMD so that you can pull out you  know slow modes medium scale modes and  fast modes and what he showed is that  you can capture intermittent and  transient phenomena for example the 