of the DMD papers  are in fluid dynamics all kinds of  applications from jets to boundary  layers combustion shock turbulent  boundary layer interactions you name it  but since then it has DMD has also been  applied to neuro  disease modeling weather and climate  robotics finance plasmas video  processing tons of applications the  common thread in all of these cases is  that you have high dimensional data  governed by a probably nonlinear process  it's multi scale in space and time and  there's coherent structures  okay there's dominant coherent patterns  and you want to pull out a linear DMD  model for those dominant patterns okay  the last thing I want to show you is a  simple example illustrating how DMD  works on a toy video problem okay so for  this example we're going to look at how  DMD works on a simple toy video this was  a video that was constructed by Bing  Brunton to demonstrate kind of how DMD  compares with principal components  analysis and independent component  analysis so this is an 80 pixel by 80  pixels image so 6,400 dimensional data  there's noise added to this and when I  click go you're gonna see that there are  these two dominant coherent patterns  that oscillate at different frequencies  so you can see there's a fast square  oscillation and a slow Gaussian and so  with without noise this is really a Rank  2 system given by these kind of  oscillating dynamics which is exactly  what DMD is tailored to pull out okay so  what does principal components do if you  just took the singular value  decomposition of this data which is  basically what principal components does  so if you took the the SPD or PCA of  this data you will in fact find that  it's a two mode system so PCA identifies  correctly the rank of the system but  because this is based entirely on energy  or kind of contained variance the modes  that come out of PCA mix these two  frequencies it doesn't pull these  frequencies apart this is the single  most energetic mode and this is the  second most energetic mode so PCA gets  their rank and that's some of the  structure but it doesn't split them up  into these coherent in time oscillations  I see a dozen  better job ica actually does extract two  shapes that correspond to the fast and  the slow dynamics but it does a pretty  poor job of denoising so this is very  very noisy and cloudy and it also  doesn't tell you exactly what the the  frequency component there's no model for  how these are evolving in time and so  finally the dynamic mode decomposition  perfectly separates out the fast and the  slow dynamics into the square and the  Skousen and not only does it separate  out these modes it gives you very clean  estimates of those modes and it  correctly identifies the frequency of  how those evolve in times so there's  this linear dynamical system model under  the hood that tells you how these modes  are going to evolve in time okay so  that's just kind of a nice intuitive  picture of what DM D is doing on a  simple example you can code this up and  try it the code is actually in our DMD  book you can download that off of our  website and try this example okay thank  you
 

